comments[7]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-07T22:53:40.707Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation (2026-11-07, branch fix/annotation-collection-reads). Every comment, note and learning row in TOON, JSON, SDK and MCP now leads with index, its absolute one-based position in the stored collection. renderAnnotationResult (src/sdk/annotations.ts) indexes the stored collection before --limit, so the index survives limits, budget pages and the pm notes --since/--event-type filters. It also indexes add and edit receipts and full-history mutation output. The index is assigned first and then reasserted, so a stored field of the same name cannot override it. The SDK types advertise IndexedAnnotationEntry. A budget-truncated listing carries total_count beside count (see pm-8s53lj).\n\nNew guard: --expect-at <ts> (SDK and MCP expectAt; the first spelling --expect-created-at widened the Commander option column and cost about 130 help tokens per command, so the flag was shortened) on --edit and --delete for comments, notes and learnings. Inside the locked mutate, a different created_at at the addressed index is refused with exit 4 and annotation_entry_mismatch before anything is written. details carries the collection, entry_index, expected and actual created_at, and matching_indexes (every index whose entry has the expected created_at). The message and the retry example name the index when exactly one entry matches. The flag is refused without --edit/--delete (annotation_expectation_without_target), and an empty value is refused as annotation_expectation_empty. The MCP schema declares expectAt and treats it as mutually exclusive with ifAbsent.\n\nNegative control (tests/unit/commands/annotations/annotation-entry-addressing.spec.ts): on a 5-comment item a --limit 2 page reports count 2. Editing by that count with the newest row's created_at is refused with entry_index 2 and matching_indexes [5], and editing the row's own index 5 succeeds.\n"
  "2026-11-08T01:19:48.076Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit full review of PR 1685 (6 threads), all addressed. (1) renderAnnotationResult indexed every stored entry before the latest-N limit. It now slices first and numbers the retained rows by their position in the complete collection (indexRetainedAnnotationEntries), so an MCP page of 20 over a long history clones only 20 rows. (2) --output-limit left a producer returned_count at the pre-limit page size. applyAmountBound now refreshes it from the delivered rows, and the tail-retention spec asserts count 2 and returned_count 2. (3) The docs now state that edit and delete without --expect-at address the absolute index unguarded, as before. They also state that created_at is a timestamp rather than a unique identity, so for entries sharing one the guard proves only the timestamp and matching_indexes lists every candidate. A stable per-entry identity stays with pm-gh1381. (4) A new spec proves that unguarded absolute-index edits remain valid: the row index addresses the newest entry, and the page count edits the older entry at index 2. (5) The MCP flat-parameter spec now asserts that every write's own result contains the new entry's text or path, and it reads notes and learnings back exactly. (6) pm-gh1684's repro steps and actual result were corrected: high_level_encoding carries 14 absent pairs and only low_level_encoding has absent [].\n"
  "2026-11-08T02:51:59.500Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit full review of the final head, findings for this item. (1) SDK_CONTEXT_INTEGRITY.md and the expectAt parameter description no longer describe the guard as proving entry identity: it compares created_at timestamps, and an index shift between entries sharing a timestamp is not refused. (2) The surface-replication waiver for src/sdk/runtime/actions.ts also suppressed that member's content check. Waivers now take an optional scope (unchanged or missing; absent covers both), this waiver is scoped to unchanged, and a gate spec proves each scope covers only its own check. (3) The suggestion to validate malformed stored entries before indexing does not apply here: such entries never reach the renderer, because sortLogValues crashes on them first with an unknown_error. That pre-existing crash, also present on main, is filed as pm-30tjoh.\n"
  "2026-11-08T03:55:05.676Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of 54e09e050, findings for this item, verified and fixed. (1) Add and edit receipts used the push or input position, but the store orders annotation entries by created_at and then text on write. A comment added after a later-stamped one (clock skew or import) was reported at index 2 with the other entry's row, and an edit that changes text among entries sharing a created_at can move the entry. runAnnotationCommand now locates the written entry after canonical ordering (locateAnnotationEntry: last entry with the same created_at, author and text) for both add and edit. (2) A single malformed stored entry (a string) skips the sort comparator, so it reached the renderer and was spread into character-numbered keys. Non-object entries are now delivered as stored, and the typed refusal stays with pm-30tjoh. (3) A nested snake_case expect_at made hoisting drop the flat expectAt without the SDK ever reading expect_at, which bypassed the guard. For comments, notes and learnings, nested snake_case spellings of declared parameters are now renamed to camelCase, and an explicit camelCase key wins. (4) The docs, JSDoc and parameter descriptions say the guard compares timestamps. The suggested JSON-schema dependency of expectAt on edit or delete was not added: the rendered schema sees only flat properties, so it would reject a valid flat expectAt with a nested options.edit, while the runtime already refuses the real case with annotation_expectation_without_target. Each fix has a regression spec, and the post-sort spec fails with the push-position index restored.\n"
  "2026-11-08T04:22:48.188Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of b08bb7cae found two P2s, both verified and fixed. (1) Locating a written entry by its created_at, author and text triple was ambiguous for twins. With b and c written in the same millisecond, editing index 1 to c reported index 2 and the untouched row without edited_at. The store's comparator (compareLogValues, now exported from src/core/item/item-format.ts and used by sortLogValues) is applied to the written array with a stable index tie-break, and the written position is followed through that order (canonicalAnnotationAddress). That is exactly the store's stable sort, so add, ifAbsent retries and edits report the occurrence they wrote, even among identical entries. (2) Passing malformed stored entries through let a stored null note reach the runNotes event filters, which dereferenced it and threw a TypeError. The filters now read note fields through storedNoteFields, which treats a non-object entry as having no fields. Specs cover twins created by an edit and a stored null note with and without event filters.\n"
  "2026-11-08T04:35:06.702Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of 6dadf4a17 found one P2, verified and fixed. runAnnotationCommand is public SDK, and an extension can pass a custom collectionKey, but the store keeps only comments, notes and learnings in canonical created_at order. So the canonical-address tracking misreported an append behind a future-dated entry in a custom collection (index 1 and the other row instead of index 2), and ifAbsent retries returned the wrong entry. The store-sorted set is now declared next to the comparator (CANONICALLY_ORDERED_LOG_COLLECTIONS in src/core/item/item-format.ts), and a custom collection keeps its write-order address. A spec drives runAnnotationCommand with collectionKey audits, and it fails with the guard removed.\n"
  "2026-11-08T05:12:19.585Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of b5fd0e9d8 (7 minor or trivial threads), all addressed. (1) An expectAt that is not a string, which MCP callers can send as a number or null, reached trim() and threw a TypeError. It is now refused as annotation_expectation_invalid, which replaces the unreleased annotation_expectation_empty and also covers an empty value. (2) The mismatch refusal's required and why text now says that the guard compares timestamps, and asks the caller to inspect the rows when several indexes share the timestamp. (3) Nested snake_case spellings of declared parameters are now renamed to camelCase for files, docs and test as well as for the annotation actions, so a nested add_glob no longer drops a flat addGlob. (4) The surface-replication report keys applied waivers by scope too, so two scoped waivers with the same owner and expiry are both reported. (5) The SDK acknowledgment and the pm-gh1657 contract line say that ordered_suffix keeps the newest rows, still listed oldest to newest within each page. The pm-gh1657 comment was corrected in place with pm comments --edit 4 --expect-at, the guard this item adds. (6) The CLI spec checks the TOON older page against the TOON cursor's own offset. (7) The MCP guard spec creates the two comments at distinct, controlled timestamps.\n"
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-08T00:39:17.966Z","harness:claude-code","A page count is never an entry address: once output can be bounded, every row must carry its absolute position, and a write by position needs an expectation that proves the position still names what was read. Also measure the help cost of a new flag's spelling before committing to it: Commander aligns every option to the widest term, so one long flag (--expect-created-at <timestamp>) re-wrapped every description and cost about 130 help tokens per command, while --expect-at <ts> cost 18."
